CAKE, with L4S marking, for routers whose Qualcomm PPE offloads the flows (qca_ppe). The hardware keeps routing, NATing and encapsulating offloaded connections, and hands their frames to a qdisc on the way out, so every flow that crosses the shaped link is shaped by CAKE: its own shaper, flow and host isolation, COBALT with ECN marking and NAT awareness, plus CE marks for L4S packets (ECT(1)) once they queue longer than 3 ms (ce_threshold, marked with a probability that rises to one at twice the threshold). Upload is shaped on the uplink device on the WAN port (pppoe-wan, a VLAN device or the port itself); download on an IFB that both the host's ingress redirect and the driver feed. Set this on the physical WAN port (wan). The per-packet overhead defaults to 50 bytes, right for PPPoE over a VLAN on Ethernet or fibre (preamble and gap, Ethernet, one VLAN tag, PPPoE); a configured link layer (linklayer, overhead, mpu) replaces it: for VDSL2 with PPPoE set overhead 34 and mpu 68 and add ptm to iqdisc_opts and eqdisc_opts, for a DHCP WAN on Ethernet overhead 44 and mpu 84. iqdisc_opts and eqdisc_opts are appended to the download and upload CAKE. The download squashes and ignores DSCP unless squash_dscp and squash_ingress say otherwise. Offloaded flows keep the DSCP a firewall rule stamps into their conntrack mark (ct mark set ip dscp or 0x80) in both directions; for the download to use it, turn squash_ingress and squash_dscp off. Without CAKE's ce_threshold in the kernel and tc, CAKE shapes as usual without the L4S mark, and the log says so. Costs CPU for each shaped packet. With use_mq, both directions run cake_mq on IFBs with a queue per CPU, so that cost spreads over all cores. With ppe_hybrid, upload flows stay in the switch, behind a hardware shaper just above the upload rate, until that shaper queues, and are handed to CAKE within a few milliseconds of it; they return once the upload has stayed below 60% of the rate for 2 seconds, so an idle or lightly used upload costs no CPU.
